A||||8||fiero56||plenum repainting||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||07-02-2000||09:16 PM||||Well, I am just about ready to start refinishing my wheels. I was looking the car over, and noticed the paint on the plenum('88 v-6)has a little white spot in back where the paint is just beginning to show its age. I have a big can of aircraft stripper. Can I use it to strip the plenum clean, and repaint it? Will this hurt the plenum? I will pull it off the engine before I start. If this is ok, what paint(spray preferrable) is an EXACT match to the stock color?||||reg||5
Z||000001||TOM||07-02-2000||09:35 PM||||Good choice on the stripper. If I were doing it I would have them powder coated.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||07-02-2000||10:08 PM||||The "paint" on the plenum is VERY tough to remove. It is so thick that it would not sandblast off. I ended up getting a large tupperware like container that would hold the intake and pouring the stripper in it and letting the stuff soak overnight. I was suprised at how rough the intake was under the thick layer of paint!||||reg||1
Z||000003||stimpy||07-02-2000||10:54 PM||||Why stay with the stock color?<P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=258191&a=1921599&p=16712481&Sequence=5"> <P>I had mine powdercoated.  The finish is very smooth and glossy. The price for stripping and refinishing was aroun $100, but I'm sure it could be done cheaper.  The powdercoating really seems to be able to shed dirt and resist discoloration. Just my 2% of a $.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||07-02-2000||11:06 PM||||Stimpy, The V6-HD looks to be applied 3D lettering. What is the material? sharp!<BR>Coy ||||reg||14
Z||000005||stimpy||07-02-2000||11:19 PM||||The V6-H.O. badge is off of the 2000 Grand Am. Check out the mall from last week, Hypnotise has a good line on them. I got mine from Mr. Goodwrench for $7. Thanx for the compliment.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||07-03-2000||11:01 AM||||I will save the plenum work for another day. <P>I bought the aircraft stripper yesterday to finally begin work on refinishing my wheels. I was told this stuff was easier than using a spray. I was reading the cautions on the can, and this is some serious sh*t. Vapors can cause blindness, cancer, etc. I would really prefer not to even open the can. Is there anything a little less dangerous? I mnight return this gallon, and buy the spray on gasket remover as Orief suggested.||||reg||5
Z||000007||HYPTNOTiSE||07-03-2000||11:49 AM||||Man when using that stuff get a paint brush to apply it, and wear the most thickest rubber gloves you can find that go all the way up your arms and wear saftly glasses. That stuff burns so badly, and water doesn't help take the burn away, you just have to sit there and suffer until it stops.  We use it work a lot, and one time the guys put it on the toilet seat as a practical joke to one of the other guys, man i'm glad i didn't have to go! WHEW!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Gokart||07-04-2000||03:15 PM||||Yup, like they said aircraft stripper is pretty evil, but does the job.  Almost as evil as muratic acid! After stripping I repainted mine myself with duplicolor high heat paint.  May not look as good as powdercoated but cost $25.00 vs $100.00 and with 3 growing boys I must improvise  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jy04$010.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR> Marc                     87GT||||reg||2
